<%# Hobbies, on HOBBIES.md's framing: the meaning-making section, not the
    fun extra. Its named trap — hobby apps that turn the hobby into
    tracking — gets disclaimed the way People disclaims contact management. %>

<section class="mt-12 max-w-3xl space-y-5">
  <p>
    This isn't the light-relief section. As work changes shape, the question
    of what you make and why you're interested stops being a hobby question
    and starts being most of the answer to what a life is for.
  </p>
  <p>
    And it's the area where the current arrangement is most obviously
    against you: the feed knows exactly what you're into, and uses that to
    keep you reading <em>about</em> the thing instead of doing it. Hours of
    woodworking videos, no wood. The counter-move here is the same as
    everywhere else in the app — own the sources, and point everything at
    doing.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="mt-14">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">What you see</h2>
  <div class="mt-6 grid grid-cols-1 gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "A page per hobby") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        Guitar gets its own corner: the practice sessions, the gear
        receipts, the people you play with, the piece you're learning, the
        notes. Not a folder — a real view across everything in your life
        that's about that one thing.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "Your own river of reading") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        The sites and writers you follow, in one chronological stream, in
        the order they published. No ranking, no recommendations, no
        engagement weighting — just what came out, from sources you chose.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "Interests you didn't declare") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        If what you actually read clusters around something you've never
        named as a hobby, the app can notice and offer to make it a proper
        one. Spotted in your own reading, on your own machine, and always
        an offer rather than a filing decision.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "The next thing to make") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        Ideas sized to your actual skill and the materials you already have,
        rather than to an algorithm's idea of an impressive project. The
        output is something you could start on Saturday.
      </p>
    <% end %>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">Tracking is not the hobby</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    Hobby apps die by making the logging into the pastime — a beautifully
    maintained record of a thing you stopped doing in March. So capture here
    is close to free, the app does the filing, and every feature is supposed
    to end in a session on the calendar, a project begun, or a person met.
    A tidier log is not the goal and is not the reward.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">The reader deserves its own note</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    Google Reader was the last mass-market software where the reading list
    was chosen entirely by the reader — and it was switched off, from
    outside, by a company that owed nobody an explanation. That is this
    whole project's argument in miniature, which is why a full feed reader
    lives in here: chronological on purpose, and working identically on an
    installation with no model configured at all.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">And the people in it</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    Most hobbies are better with somebody else doing them too, and most
    adult friendships now start around a shared interest rather than
    proximity. So the hobby pages point outward — where the people who do
    this gather, and how to actually turn up — instead of treating a hobby
    as a solitary hardware collection.
  </p>
</section>
